      "year": 2020,
      "abstract": "Kant\u2019s Nonideal Theory of Politics is a very welcome addition to the growing roster of recent studies of Kant\u2019s politics. Unlike many, which concentrate on a particular policy area (such as colonialism or just war theory), Huseyinzadegan\u2019s aim is a systematic one: namely, to sketch out a hitherto underspecified \u2018non-ideal\u2019 political realm to complement the ideal theory for which Kant is best known. Her most original claims centre on what she calls \u2018political Zweckm\u00e4ssigkeit\u2019, for on her account, the main rules that are to guide the political actor in non-ideal circumstances are the regulative teleological principles elaborated in the first and third Critiques, as well as occasional writings, including, most famously, the Idea for a Universal History, What is it to Orient Oneself in Thinking, and Perpetual Peace. By linking political action in a non-ideal world with such familiar critical principles she hopes not only to situate such action within Kant\u2019s larger critical framework, but also to provide a ready means of disassociating Kantianism rightly understood fromhis less savoury positions on gender, race and Eurocentric history. Huseyinzadegan especially associates such unfortunate tendencies of Kant\u2019s thought with his embrace of a Eurocentric \u2018cosmopolitanism\u2019, which she sharply distinguishes from \u2018peace\u2019, as the object of ideal politics. Unlike \u2018peace\u2019, cosmopolitanism in a political, as distinguished from strictly moral, sense is, on her reading, a revisable regulative ideal that guides Kant\u2019s own non-ideal politics as it stands, but that is also deeply compromised by Eurocentrism and na\u00efve assumptions as to the benignity of \u2018commerce\u2019. The aim of her effort is not only to salvage what is worth keeping in Kant\u2019s ideal theory but also to open a path toward a revised version of non-ideal politics (not offered in Huseyinzadegan\u2019s current study) in which the goals of Kant\u2019s ideal theory might be pursued through regulative principles that were both more respectful of human diversity and fallibility and more mindful of both the dark side of commerce and the ongoing burden BOOK REVIEWS",
